lt to do and in that  clip you showed one of the key words was  aspire I aspire to manage  compassionately because it can be so  difficult to get out of your own head  and be a spectator to your own thoughts  especially when you become emotional  good successful companies have core  values that everybody understands how  did you put that value into practice  yeah it's a great question so it's one  thing to codify your values and I think  increasingly companies are thinking  through  what their first principles are it's  another to actually walk the walk and so  for us at LinkedIn it starts with the  recruiting process and not lowering or  compromising the bar in terms of our the  importance of our values that continues  on with onboarding and the first day  someone sets foot within the company  we're going to start to help educate  them on what's most important to us and  not only what we're trying to accomplish  but how we're trying to accomplish it  which i think is increasingly important  we do learning and development around  our culture and values we evaluate  performance around culture and values  what about taking it from the other end  of the telescope say I work for somebody  who shows none of those things I think  it depends on personal situation I think  it depends on the company I don't think  there's any cookie cutter model for this  and to be frank there's companies and  there's leaders who've had extraordinary  success with not managing compassion you  also talked in your speech about the  growing economic divide and tribalism in  the job market what do you mean by that  and how how that really is changing the  world we live in today yeah two  different components of this and why I  believe that compassion will grow in its  importance one is widening  socio-economic stratification the the  gap between the haves and have-nots here  in the States is hovering near  historical highs and when you have a lot  of wealth concentrated amongst the  smaller group of people and you have  broad swaths and segments of the  population that don't feel like they  have equal access to Economic  Opportunity no matter how hard they're  working it's bad for the economy because  they can't afford products and services  it's bad for society because they feel  disenfranchised so interesting to see  the LinkedIn's data shows the number one  skill lacking mo markers is  interpersonal or soft skills how's that  impacting the job market so there it's  interesting because a lot of people are  fixated on technology and rightfully so  it's an increasingly important part of  most companies and how they do business  but what we found when we did our skills  gap analytical work is that  interpersonal skills the gap there is  roughly three times higher than software  engineering in the United States when we  talk about interpersonal skills we're  talking about skills that anyone can  develop and acquire talking about  communication reasoning team  coordination jobs and customer service  sales development business development  education all require those kinds of  skills you say anyone can acquire them  but where  go acquire them if you're somebody  looking at the job market and given the  shortages in the job market now how long  is gonna take somebody to do or take the  job market to catch up with the jobs  that are needed right now yeah so  thankfully again it's not just these  technical skills that could require  longer periods of time to develop these  kinds of interpersonal skills you can  pick up in classes that are online  increasingly and it's wonderful to see  that there's a rise in the amount of  courses being offered and the number of  providers who are increasingly thinking  about where the jobs are and will be and  the skills required to obtain those jobs  but we're also hearing there's a surplus  of jobs but employers are having trouble  filling them yeah we have an answer to  that  according McKinsey roughly 40 percent of  employer